    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "I've made the change, and all 4 acceptance tests pass on `bin/e2e`.\n\n**What changed:** a claim's terms are now looked up on the sheet's today at the moment the claim is created, not on the claim's date. That covers the person's own terms, the grade they hold that day, and the grade and sheet terms in force that day. The due-by is still counted from the claim's date, with holidays applied as before.\n\nThe fix is in `src/main/java/net/officefloor/hq/app/claim/ClaimService.java`:\n- **Adding a claim** (`addClaim`, about line 145) now uses the sheet's today for the lookup. Every way of creating a claim goes through this method: the claim form, paste, templates, quick add and plans. I also updated its doc comment to say so.\n- **Splitting a claim** (about line 608) creates a new claim now, so I gave it the same rule: its terms are those in force on the day of the split. The tests don't cover splitting. If a split part should instead keep the original claim's terms, it's a one-line change.\n\nNothing else needed changing:\n- The front end only displays the stored due-by.\n- When a holiday is added later, due-bys are recalculated from the terms stored on each claim, so they stay consistent with the new rule.\n\n**Not done: existing stored claims.** Claims already in a database keep the due-bys they were given under the old rule; I didn't add a migration to recalculate them. One could use each claim's first status-log entry, which records the sheet's today when the claim was created. But moving due-bys on old claims could shift them into closed periods, so I'd rather you decide whether you want that.",
